so one of the assumptions is that  humanitarian aid workers often work in  the field which is um  we need to clarify a bit first what is  actually the field the field can be many  things  if you look from from headquarters in in  brussels or geneva the field can already  be  kabul or bangy or another capital city  well if you sit in kabul in an office  then provincial or even a smaller town  can be the field  and for many of us work will happen in  offices work will be behind a computer  not  every one of us will be out in let's say  refugee camps or  on on difficult border crossing stations  where where people  are are crossing under difficult  circumstances  so many of us will will not be in what  immediately would be perceived as  the field and yet we are all doing  humanitarian work there  i think one of the important things to  keep in mind is also if you really want  to work in  let's call it the field um is how  well are you able to work there how well  do you fit in do you  do you do you know the cultural codes do  you know the language are you actually  able to be of an added value where you  are  and i think this is one of the very  important reflections people  always need to have when they want to go  into humanitarian work  is where do i bring an added value  you
 

